// Lava is the heat verb: the first thing in the world that is hot *because
// of what it is* rather than because something set it on fire. Everything
// before it needed a flame -- oil had to be lit, wood had to be next to
// something already burning -- so there was no way to *apply* heat to a
// scene. Pour this instead.
//
// # The cooling model (which replaced a pin)
//
// A lava cell is **born** at `intrinsic_temperature` on its first visit,
// then cools through its own (deliberately tiny) `heat_conductivity` and
// crusts into stone at `cooling_point` -- the same downward phase
// machinery water -> ice uses. The emergent behaviour is the point: edge
// cells against air lose heat fast and skin over, interior cells feel
// almost no pull and stay searing, so a flow advances under its own crust
// and a stranded film -- all edge, no interior -- is the first thing to
// freeze. A finished flow is stone all the way down, and its chunks
// sleep.
//
// The first shipped model *pinned* the cell to 1000C on every visit
// instead, and its cost was measured before being paid twice: a pinned
// cell can never cool, so every scrap a flow stranded where water
// couldn't reach stayed molten forever -- `filmstrip scene=lavapour` held
// 195 molten cells at frame 1500 and 143 at 3000 with 9 of 40 chunks
// permanently awake and the pond below simmering at ~25 boil/condense
// pairs a frame with nothing left flowing. Do not bring the pin back for
// another material: any always-off-ambient cell holds its chunk awake for
// as long as it exists (`fire::must_stay_dirty`), which is ruinous for
// anything that can be stranded. A born-hot material must also set a
// `cooling_point` above ambient -- `Material::from` asserts it at load --
// or it would cool to the exact ambient default and be re-born forever.
//
// 1000C is chosen against the two thresholds it has to clear, not from a
// basalt table: it is far above water's 100C boiling point (so a pool near
// lava boils rather than merely warming) and far enough above the 400C
// `wood.ron` now autoignites at that a log lying *against* a flow lights
// without needing a flame first -- though a single wood cell brushed by a
// single lava cell does not, which is deliberate; see wood.ron for the
// arithmetic. `render.rs`'s heat glow saturates at 400C above ambient,
// so anything from ~420C up draws identically -- the number is a
// simulation statement, not a colour one.
//
// # Quench
//
// The reaction lives **here, on lava**, never on `water.ron`, and that is
// a performance contract rather than a style preference:
// `fire::update`'s thermally-inert fast path tests `reactions.is_empty()`,
// and `try_react` clones the reaction `Vec` once per visited cell that has
// one. Water is one of the most numerous materials in any scene, so a
// reaction listed on water would be paid for by every water cell in the
// world; lava is a handful of cells. Same pair, same outcome, opposite
// cost.
//
// `produces: ("stone", "steam")` is ordered `(self, with)` -- lava becomes
// stone, the water it touched becomes steam. Both take the hotter side's
// temperature (see `fire::try_react`), so the steam is born at ~1000C and
// rises as a real plume instead of flashing straight back to water under
// its own 45C condensation point.
//
// chance 0.8, not 1.0: at 1.0 a lava front meeting a pool converts a whole
// contact row in a single frame and the interface reads as a drawn line.
// Below 1 the crust stipples in over a few frames, which is the same
// argument `fire::PHASE_CHANGE_CHANCE` makes for a boiling surface.
//
// # Tried and reverted: a minimum fill on the reactant
//
// The reaction converts a *whole* neighbour cell, and a flow splits into
// partial-fill cells on the way down, so the obvious reading is that a
// thinned-out flow quenches more water than it is worth: an 800-cell blob
// dropped into a pond fires ~944 reactions. A `min_fill` gate on the
// reactant was built, swept at 250 / 500 / 750 on `filmstrip
// scene=lavadrop` (rebuilding between points), and **it does not reduce
// the water loss at all** -- it only moves it between mechanisms:
//
// | min_fill | reacted | boiled | froze | standing water |
// |---|---|---|---|---|
// | 0 | 944 | 33 | 5 | 11,021 |
// | 250 | 820 | 181 | 122 | 10,988 |
// | 500 | 810 | 193 | 131 | 10,983 |
// | 750 | 781 | 208 | 152 | 10,995 |
//
// The thin lava that no longer quenches crusts instead, and the water it
// would have flashed boils a moment later off the hot stone. Reverted, and
// recorded here because the reasoning that motivates it is sound and will
// occur to someone again.
//
// The premise was also weaker than it looked once measured: 944 reactions
// for 800 cells of lava is 1.18 water per lava, and the whole event banks
// 924 cell-equivalents -- close to one cell of water per cell of lava,
// which is the ratio that was wanted. What made it *look* like four times
// that was a separate bug: the quench crust used to lid the pond instead
// of sinking, and the boil/condense loop churned underneath it.
//
// # Stone does not melt back
//
// **Deliberately off**: `stone.ron` has no `melting_point`/`melts_into`,
// so there is no stone -> lava direction and the cycle is one-way. Adding
// it is a one-line content change, and it is left undone on purpose --
// stone is the single most numerous solid in the world, and a finite
// `melting_point` takes every stone cell off the thermally-inert fast
// path for a transition that essentially never fires (nothing in the world
// reaches 1200C except lava itself, which is already stone's *product*).
// Revisit when there is a heat source that is meant to melt terrain.
(
    name: "lava",
    display: "Lava",
    kind: Liquid,
    // Molten rock, and the number does real work: 2.7 sinks through water
    // (1.0) and through sand (1.6), so lava poured into a pond goes to the
    // bottom and quenches from below rather than skating across the
    // surface. Stone is `Solid` and never displaced regardless.
    density: 2.7,
    // Viscous. Water runs at 1000 (i.e. uncapped -- see water.ron for why
    // that is deliberate there); lava is the material that should visibly
    // *crawl*, and this is the knob that says so.
    //
    // Swept 20/40/80 on `filmstrip scene=lavapour`, rebuilding between
    // points (this file is `include_str!`'d -- identical output across a
    // sweep means the knob was never connected; these three differ, so it
    // was). **The honest result is that the three are hard to tell apart by
    // eye on that scene**, because its ramp is steep enough that the pour
    // is dominated by falling rather than by horizontal transfer, and
    // flow_rate only governs the latter. What did separate them was the
    // quench count: `reacted` at frame 1500 came to 749 / 803 / 685 for
    // 20 / 40 / 80, i.e. 40 delivers the most lava into the water over the
    // run, with 20 leaving a thicker residual film stalled on the ramp and
    // 80 thinning the front out. Kept at 40 on that, not on a look
    // judgement nobody could actually make from the sheet. Re-sweep it on a
    // *flat* pour, where horizontal transfer is the whole story, if the
    // crawl ever needs to be tuned for its own sake.
    flow_rate: 40,
    // Well above water's 16: lava is allowed to stop levelling while still
    // visibly uneven, which is both what molten rock looks like and one
    // less pool shuffling fill forever to keep its chunks awake (a pinned
    // material's chunks are expensive to keep awake -- see the note above).
    //
    // What it trades, since two knobs here move the same number in
    // different currencies: this is also the knob that decides how thick
    // the residual film stranded on a slope is, because horizontal
    // transfer stops exactly when neighbouring fills come within it.
    // Under the pin that film was a permanent cost (every stranded cell
    // stayed molten forever); under the cooling model it is a look --
    // the film crusts into the stone scab the flow leaves along its path,
    // and this number is how thick that scab runs.
    min_transfer: 60,
    // Explicit, against the surrounding default: lava must not dry up in
    // the open the way a puddle does. It leaves by quenching or by
    // crusting, never by evaporating.
    evaporates: false,
    // This is lava's *cooling rate*, and its smallness is the whole
    // character of the material -- it does NOT throttle how fast lava
    // heats other things (each cell warms using its own conductivity, so
    // wood at 0.15 and water at 0.08 heat just as fast off a 0.02 lava
    // cell as off a 0.2 one). What it decides is how long lava itself
    // stays molten: an edge cell against open air loses roughly
    // (neighbour_avg - 1000) * 0.02 ≈ 10C a visit and crusts in tens of
    // frames, while an interior cell surrounded by other lava feels
    // almost no pull and stays searing until the flow thins -- so a pour
    // skins over at its face and stays molten in the body, and a stranded
    // film (all edge, no body) is the *first* thing to freeze rather than
    // the thing that never does.
    heat_conductivity: 0.002,
    // Born at 1000C -- once, on first visit, not re-pinned (see
    // `MaterialDef::intrinsic_temperature` for the measured cost of the
    // pin this replaced: 195 stranded molten cells and 9/40 chunks awake
    // forever on `scene=lavapour`). From birth it only cools.
    intrinsic_temperature: 1000.0,
    // The cooling model's floor: below 700C molten rock stops being
    // molten. Uses the same downward phase machinery as water -> ice,
    // stipple-gated per visit, so a cooling front crusts in over a few
    // frames rather than drawing a line. Stone is born at ~700 and hands
    // its remaining heat back through its own conductivity, so a finished
    // flow's chunks genuinely sleep.
    cooling_point: 700.0,
    cools_into: "stone",
    // Zero, against water's 900 default: the fill gate exists to conserve
    // volume around the freeze/thaw *loop*, and lava -> stone is one-way
    // (stone never melts back). A stranded partial-fill film crusting into
    // a thin stone scab along the slope is the look a finished flow owes,
    // and the modest volume gain becomes inert terrain rather than
    // circulating water.
    freeze_min_fill: 0,
    reactions: [
        (with: "water", produces: ("stone", "steam"), chance: 0.8),
    ],
    // Deep reds and oranges rather than the bright yellow lava is usually
    // drawn as: `render.rs`'s heat glow already blends a non-burning cell
    // halfway toward its bright `FIRE_TINT_HIGH` (255, 210, 110) at this
    // temperature, so a palette that started bright would clip to a flat
    // cream. Starting dark leaves the glow somewhere to travel, and leaves
    // the per-cell shade grain visible through it.
    colors: [
        (198, 72, 24),
        (176, 52, 18),
        (214, 96, 30),
        (160, 40, 14),
    ],
)
